Goal: Understand the molecular cause behind the mutant dumpy phenotype in Drosophila melanogaster.
Step 1: Transferring the sequences to the terminal
I already have the sequences (fasta files) stored on my device from my genetics lab work. These sequences were obtained through DNA extraction and purification.
In order to perform alignment and compare both the wild-type and mutant fruit fly DNA sequences, I must first transfer the sequences to the terminal. 
For a file named "Reference.gb," go to the Windows powershell and use the following command:
  scp "C:\Users\prasa\Downloads\Reference.gb" gprasad3@sabine.rcdc.uh.edu:/project/stuckert/gprasad3/Final_project/
I repeated the same for my mutant sequence file saved as "mutant.fasta."

Step 2: Converting the gb file to a fasta file
Since I will be using MAFFT for alignment, I need to convert my reference file from a GenBank format to the standard fasta format.
This can be done using SeqIO, which is under Biopython.
First, download Biopython if you haven't already:
  pip install --user biopython
Then, we can use SeqIO to convert the file:
  python3 -c "from Bio import SeqIO; SeqIO.convert('Reference.gb', 'genbank', 'Reference.fasta', 'fasta')"
*Note that my input file was named Reference.gb and the SeqIO command was run inside the same directory where the file was located.

Step 3: Removing hidden return characters
Since the files were uploaded to the terminal from Windows, it might contain hidden return characters that could cause errors when using MAFFT. 
These characters can be removed using dos2unix:
  dos2unix Reference.fasta
I ran the same step for my other file "mutant.fasta" as well.

Step 4: Running the alignment
MAFFT was already downloaded, so all I had to do was include its absolute path when running the command.
  /project/stuckert/bioinformatics/software/mafft-7.525-without-extensions/bin/mafft --auto combined.fasta > Aligned.fasta
*Exporting the path to MAFFT beforehand is also an option.

Step 5: Checking the alignment results
Once the alignment was complete, I checked the output file "Aligned.fasta" using the following commands:
  grep ">" Aligned.fasta
Note that this gave me two lines, one for the header of the mutant sequence and one for the header of the wild-type sequence.
    less Aligned.fasta
Using this command, I was able to scroll through the whole file and find regions of mismatch, indicated using dashes "-" on the sequence.

Step 6: Making a visual on R studio
In order to visualize the mismatch, I used R studio. 
First, I had to download the file onto my computer. So I went to Windows powershell and typed:
  scp gprasad3@sabine.rcdc.uh.edu:/project/stuckert/gprasad3/Final_project/Aligned.fasta C:\Users\prasa\Documents\
Now I can open the file on R studio.
Because the dumpy mutation is a very large (around 7.4 kb) insertion, I chose to make a visualization for the 100 bp at the beginning and end regions of the insertion.
  library(ggmsa)
  ggmsa("C:/Users/prasa/Downloads/Aligned.fasta", 
                 start = 47700, 
                 end = 47800, 
                 seq_name = TRUE, 
                 color = "Chemistry_NT")
This created a plot at the beginning site of the insertion, which I saved onto my device.
I did the same for the end range by changing the start point to 55100 and the end point to 55200.

Step 7: Using Blastn to better visualize the alignment mismatch
I wanted a better way to see the where the insertion occurs, so I used blastn for a second alignment.
I downloaded blast first:
  wget https://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/blast/executables/blast+/2.16.0/ncbi-blast-2.16.0+-x64-linux.tar.gz
  tar -xzf ncbi-blast-2.16.0+-x64-linux.tar.gz
Now you can run blastn:
  ./ncbi-blast-2.16.0+/bin/blastn -query mutant.fasta -subject Reference.fasta -outfmt 0 -out blast_output.txt
  cat blast_output.txt
This showed me that the query matched the reference in two separate chunks, one from 1-685 and the second chunk from 8095-8470. 
The huge region that was missing in the alignment from 685-8095 was where the insertion occurred, spanning approximately 7410 bases.

